function pid_write_results(mdl, tg, spec_lon, spec_lat, gl, ga, V, chk, sweep, naive, R, Rv, Rl, Rg, AW, NL, dchk)
%PID_WRITE_RESULTS  Write RESULTS.md (all numbers come from pid_main.m variables).
T = mdl.T; par = mdl.par;
fid = fopen('RESULTS.md', 'w');
fp = @(varargin) fprintf(fid, varargin{:});
fl = @(s) fprintf(fid, '%s\n', s);
PF_ = {'FAIL', 'PASS'}; ps = @(c) PF_{double(c) + 1};
cplx = @(z) strtrim(sprintf('%.5f%+.5fi, ', [real(z(:)) imag(z(:))].'));
a = -mdl.Gv.den(2); b = mdl.Gv.num(2); a1 = mdl.Gr.den(2); a2 = mdl.Gr.den(3); br = mdl.Gr.num(end);

fl('# 02 Pole-placement discrete PID - RESULTS');
fl('');
fp('Generated by `pid_main.m` (sampling time T = %.2f s everywhere; every number below is printed by the script). Octave 8.4 run, code written for MATLAB R2026a compatibility (no local functions in scripts).\n\n', T);
fl('Summary: two analytic pole-placement PIDs (longitudinal position loop, lateral cross-track loop) with a filtered derivative, back-calculation anti-windup, a zero-cancelling reference prefilter (2-DOF) and speed scheduling of the lateral gains. All targets (zeta >= 0.7, t_ss <= 2.5 s, OS < 10 %) are met on the full-order identified models and on the non-linear plant.');
fl('');

fl('## 1. Design plants (discretisation stated exactly)');
fl('');
fp('Identified models (01_sysid/RESULTS.md): `Gv(z) = b/(z - a)`, a = %.6f, b = %.6f (v_cmd -> v, DC gain %.4f); `Gr(z) = br z^-3 / (1 + a1 z^-1 + a2 z^-2) = br / (z (z^2 + a1 z + a2))`, br = %.6f, a1 = %.6f, a2 = %.6f (u_lat -> yaw rate r, DC gain k_delta = %.4f).\n\n', a, b, mdl.Gv.dcgain, br, a1, a2, mdl.Gr.dcgain);
fl('**Longitudinal loop** (output s = along-track position, control v_cmd). Speed is integrated with forward Euler, `s(k+1) = s(k) + T v(k)`, i.e. `T/(z-1)`:');
fl('```');
fl('Gs(z) = Gv(z) T/(z-1) = b T / ((z - a)(z - 1))        (order 2, exact; type 1)');
fl('```');
fl('**Lateral loop** (output e_y = cross-track error, left positive; control u_lat = yaw-rate demand). Linearised path following at design speed **v0 = 2.0 m/s**: `psi_e(k+1) = psi_e(k) + T r(k)`, `e_y(k+1) = e_y(k) + T v0 psi_e(k)` (forward Euler):');
fl('```');
fl('G_ey(z) = v0 T^2 Gr(z)/(z-1)^2 = v0 T^2 br / ( z (z^2 + a1 z + a2) (z-1)^2 )   (order 5, 3-sample delay, type 2)');
fl('```');
fl('The PID error is e = e_ref - e_y, so the plant gain is positive in both loops. The **full-order** plants are used for the design and for all verifications (no model reduction in the final design; see Section 4.3 for what a reduction would cost).');
fl('');

fl('## 2. Discrete PID: exact form');
fl('');
fl('Parallel form, backward-Euler integrator, filtered backward-difference derivative (one extra parameter N, `pf = 1/(1 + N T)` = pole of the derivative filter; `D(k)` is the backward-Euler discretisation of `Kd s/(1 + s/N)`):');
fl('```');
fl('I(k)  = I(k-1) + Ki T e(k)');
fl('D(k)  = pf D(k-1) + Kd (1 - pf)/T * (e(k) - e(k-1))');
fl('u(k)  = Kp e(k) + I(k) + D(k)                      (before saturation; implemented in pid_step.m)');
fl('C(z)  = Kp + Ki T z/(z-1) + Kdf (z-1)/(z-pf) = Nc(z) / ((z-1)(z-pf)),   Kdf := Kd (1-pf)/T');
fl('Nc(z) = c2 z^2 + c1 z + c0,');
fl('  c2 = Kp + Ki T + Kdf,   c1 = -[(1+pf) Kp + pf Ki T + 2 Kdf],   c0 = pf Kp + Kdf');
fl('```');
fl('With pf = 0 this is the textbook `Kp + Ki T z/(z-1) + Kd (z-1)/(T z)`. The four free parameters are (Kp, Ki, Kd, pf) <-> (c2, c1, c0, pf).');
fl('');

fl('## 3. Desired closed-loop poles (analytic from the specs)');
fl('');
fl('```');
fl('2 % settling time        t_ss  ~= 4/(zeta wn)               -> wn = 4/(zeta t_ss,design)');
fl('overshoot of a 2nd-order pair   OS = exp(-pi zeta/sqrt(1-zeta^2))   (zeta = 0.70: 4.6 %, 0.75: 2.8 %, 0.85: 0.6 %)');
fl('dominant pair   s_{1,2} = -zeta wn +/- j wn sqrt(1-zeta^2)');
fl('extra poles     s_{3,4} = -kappa zeta wn  (real, double)    -> kappa = 4 (lon), 2 (lat)');
fl('mapping         z_i = exp(s_i T)                           (exact for the sampled system)');
fl('```');
sl = log(gl.desired_poles)/T; sa = log(ga.desired_poles)/T;
fp('| loop | zeta | t_ss design [s] | wn [rad/s] | kappa | s-poles [1/s] | z-poles |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n');
fp('| lon | %.2f | %.2f | %.4f | %.0f | %s | %s |\n', gl.zeta, gl.ts_dom, gl.wn, gl.kappa(1), strtrim(sprintf('%.3f%+.3fi, ', [real(sl) imag(sl)].')), cplx(gl.desired_poles));
fp('| lat | %.2f | %.2f | %.4f | %.0f | %s | %s |\n\n', ga.zeta, ga.ts_dom, ga.wn, ga.kappa(1), strtrim(sprintf('%.3f%+.3fi, ', [real(sa) imag(sa)].')), cplx(ga.desired_poles));
fl('**Why the lateral extra poles are only 2x (not 4-5x) faster.** The path-following plant has two integrators, a 3-sample delay and a lightly damped 2nd-order lag (wn = 24.7 rad/s). The controller denominator `(z-1)(z-pf)` fixes the trace of the closed-loop pole set: `sum(all 7 poles) = sum(plant poles) + 1 + pf` (the top coefficient of Delta(z) does not depend on Kp, Ki, Kd), so pf is tied to the sum of the assigned and the residual poles. Solving the linear Diophantine system for faster extra poles (kappa >= 3) returns pf > 1 - an unrealisable, unstable derivative filter - whereas for the longitudinal plant (order 2, no residual poles) kappa = 4 is fine. Design-space check on the **full** model (script output):');
fl('');
fl('| case | pf | max abs(pole) | zeta_dom | OS % (prefilter) | t_ss [s] |');
fl('|---|---|---|---|---|---|');
for i = 1:numel(sweep)
    s_ = sweep(i);
    if isnan(s_.ts), fp('| %s | %.3f | %.3f | %.2f | unstable / unrealisable | - |\n', s_.label, s_.pf, s_.maxp, s_.zd);
    else, fp('| %s | %.3f | %.4f | %.2f | %.2f | %.2f |\n', s_.label, s_.pf, s_.maxp, s_.zd, s_.os, s_.ts); end
end
fl('');
fp('Reading: zeta trades damping against speed - the achieved 2 %% settling time of the lateral loop grows from %.2f s (zeta = 0.70) to %.2f s (0.90). zeta = 0.75 (>= 0.7 with margin) with kappa = 2 keeps t_ss well inside 2.5 s with a realisable derivative filter pole pf = %.3f (N = %.0f rad/s, filter time constant %.1f ms) - the choice is made on the specification (t_ss, zeta); the gains then follow analytically.\n\n', sweep(1).ts, sweep(5).ts, ga.pf, ga.N, -1000*T/log(ga.pf));
fl('');

fl('## 4. Characteristic polynomial and coefficient matching');
fl('');
fl('### 4.1 Closed-loop polynomial');
fl('```');
fl('plant G = Np(z)/Dp(z),  Dp monic of degree n (n = 2 lon, n = 5 lat),  Np = const (lon: b T; lat: v0 T^2 br)');
fl('1 + C G = 0  <=>  Delta(z) = (z-1)(z-pf) Dp(z) + Nc(z) Np(z) = 0,       degree n+2');
fl('Delta(1) = Nc(1) Np(1)   (Dc(1) = 0)');
fl('```');
fl('### 4.2 Longitudinal loop (n = 2): symbolic derivation, exact 4x4 matching');
fl('With `Dp = z^2 + p1 z + p0`, `p1 = -(1+a)`, `p0 = a`, `n0 = b T` and the desired polynomial `Ad(z) = prod (z - z_i) = z^4 + e3 z^3 + e2 z^2 + e1 z + e0`:');
fl('```');
fl('(z-1)(z-pf)(z^2 + p1 z + p0)  =  z^4 + [p1 - (1+pf)] z^3 + [p0 - (1+pf) p1 + pf] z^2 + [pf p1 - (1+pf) p0] z + pf p0');
fl('Delta(z) = that + n0 (c2 z^2 + c1 z + c0)');
fl('matching with Ad(z):');
fl('  z^3 :  p1 - 1 - pf               = e3     =>  pf = p1 - 1 - e3 = sum(z_i) - 2 - a');
fl('  z^2 :  p0 - (1+pf) p1 + pf + n0 c2 = e2   =>  c2 = [e2 - p0 + (1+pf) p1 - pf]/n0');
fl('  z^1 :  pf p1 - (1+pf) p0 + n0 c1   = e1   =>  c1 = [e1 - pf p1 + (1+pf) p0]/n0');
fl('  z^0 :  pf p0 + n0 c0               = e0   =>  c0 = [e0 - pf p0]/n0');
fl('```');
fl('The PID gains follow from the linear 3x3 system (det = -(1-pf)^2 != 0):');
fl('```');
fl('[ 1     1    1 ] [Kp  ]   [ c2 ]');
fl('[1+pf  pf   2 ] [Ki T] = [-c1 ]            =>   Kp  = [-c1 - pf c2 - (2-pf) c0]/(1-pf)^2');
fl('[ pf    0    1 ] [Kdf ]   [ c0 ]                 Kdf = c0 - pf Kp,   Ki T = c2 - c0 - (1-pf) Kp,   Kd = Kdf T/(1-pf)');
fl('```');
fp('Numbers (lon): Ad = z^4 %+.6f z^3 %+.6f z^2 %+.6f z %+.6f; p1 = %.6f, p0 = %.6f, n0 = %.6f -> pf = %.6f, Nc = [%.5f %.5f %.5f]. Closed-form formulas vs generic Diophantine solver: max difference %.1e (3x3 solve check %.1e).\n\n', ...
    gl.desired_poly(2), gl.desired_poly(3), gl.desired_poly(4), gl.desired_poly(5), -(1+a), a, b*T, gl.pf, gl.Nc, max(abs([dchk.Kp dchk.Ki dchk.Kd dchk.pf] - [gl.Kp gl.Ki gl.Kd gl.pf])), dchk.solve_check);
fl('The correctness of the expansion is checked numerically in the script: all four desired poles are roots of `Delta(z)` built by polynomial multiplication (max distance in Section 6), and the poles of `feedback(C G, 1)` from the Control System Toolbox agree (difference printed in Section 6).');
fl('');
fl('### 4.3 Lateral loop (n = 5): Diophantine equation on the FULL model');
fl('A 3-parameter (4 with the filter) PID cannot place all n+2 = 7 poles of the full path-following model. Instead of discarding dynamics, the **dominant pair and the two extra real poles are placed exactly on the full model** and the remaining n-2 = 3 poles are the roots of an unknown monic residual polynomial R(z):');
fl('```');
fl('(z-1)(z-pf) Dp(z) + Nc(z) Np  =  Ad(z) R(z),     R(z) = z^3 + r2 z^2 + r1 z + r0');
fl('<=>  z(z-1) Dp(z) + pf (1-z) Dp(z) + (c2 z^2 + c1 z + c0) Np = Ad(z)(z^3 + r2 z^2 + r1 z + r0)     [(z-1)(z-pf) = z(z-1) + pf(1-z)]');
fl('```');
fl('Delta is affine in (pf, c2, c1, c0) and R is affine in (r2, r1, r0), so matching the n+2 = 7 non-leading coefficients is a **square linear system** `A x = b`, `x = [pf c2 c1 c0 r0 r1 r2]^T` (`pid_diophantine.m`; for n = 2 it degenerates to the equations of 4.2). Gains follow from the same closed form as in 4.2.');
fp('Numbers (lat): residual |A x - b| = %.1e, cond(A) = %.1e; pf = %.6f; Nc = [%.5f %.5f %.5f]; residual polynomial roots (poles NOT assigned, all verified inside the unit circle): %s (these are fast: |z| <= %.2f, i.e. >= %.0f rad/s).\n\n', ...
    ga.dioph_resid, ga.dioph_cond, ga.pf, ga.Nc, cplx(ga.R_poles), max(abs(ga.R_poles)), min(-log(abs(ga.R_poles(abs(ga.R_poles) > 1e-3)))/T));
fl('**Why not the static reduction?** The assignment hint (reduce Gr to its DC behaviour k_delta, place the poles on `k_delta v0 T^2/(z-1)^2` by exact matching with `pid_coeffmatch.m`) was implemented and tested on the full model with the same desired poles:');
fp('| design | Kp | Ki | Kd | pf | full-model max abs(pole) | min zeta of all poles | zeta_dom | t_ss prefilter [s] |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n');
fp('| static reduction (DC gain only) | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f | %.4f | %.2f | %.2f | %.2f |\n', naive.Kp, naive.Ki, naive.Kd, naive.pf, naive.maxp, naive.zmin, naive.zdom, naive.ts);
fp('| **Diophantine on full model (used)** | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f | %.4f | %.2f | %.2f | %.2f |\n\n', ga.Kp, ga.Ki, ga.Kd, ga.pf, max(abs(V.lat.poles)), V.lat.zeta_min_all, V.lat.zeta_dom, V.lat.m2.ts);
fp('The neglected 0.1 s equivalent delay (3-sample delay + servo lag) collapses the damping of the dominant pole pair from the designed 0.75 to %.2f with the reduced-model gains; hence the reduction was rejected and the exact full-order placement is used.\n\n', naive.zdom);

fl('## 5. Resulting gains');
fl('');
fl('| loop | Kp | Ki [1/s] | Kd [s] | pf | N [rad/s] | Ti=Kp/Ki [s] | Td=Kd/Kp [s] | Kt (anti-windup) [1/s] |');
fl('|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|');
fp('| lon (v_cmd [m/s] per position error [m]) | %.5f | %.5f | %.5f | %.5f | %.2f | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\n', gl.Kp, gl.Ki, gl.Kd, gl.pf, gl.N, gl.Ti, gl.Td, gl.Kt);
fp('| lat at v0 = 2 m/s (u_lat [rad/s] per cross-track error [m]) | %.5f | %.5f | %.5f | %.5f | %.2f | %.3f | %.3f | %.3f |\n\n', ga.Kp, ga.Ki, ga.Kd, ga.pf, ga.N, ga.Ti, ga.Td, ga.Kt);
fp('PID numerator Nc(z) (zeros = prefilter poles): lon [%.5f %.5f %.5f], zeros %s; lat [%.5f %.5f %.5f], zeros %s.\n\n', gl.Nc, cplx(V.lon.zeros), ga.Nc, cplx(V.lat.zeros));
fl('**Lateral speed scheduling.** The plant gain is proportional to v (`e_y'' = v psi_e`). Scaling Kp, Ki, Kd by v0/v keeps the loop transfer function - and therefore every closed-loop pole of the full model - identical for any speed (checked: characteristic polynomial difference 0 for v = 1, 2, 3 m/s). `pid_sched(g, v, vmin)` does this (v clipped below at vmin = 1 m/s, the lower end of the identified range).');
fl('');

fl('## 6. Verification on the full-order linear models');
fl('');
fp('Achieved closed-loop poles (roots of Delta, full order; lon: 4, lat: 7):\n\n- lon: %s\n- lat: %s\n\n', cplx(V.lon.poles), cplx(V.lat.poles));
fp('Maximum distance desired -> achieved pole: lon %.1e, lat %.1e (numerical root sensitivity of double poles, ~sqrt(eps)); Control System Toolbox cross-check `pole(feedback(C*G,1))`: lon %.1e, lat %.1e (NaN = toolbox absent). Dominant achieved poles: lon s = %.3f %+.3fi 1/s -> zeta = %.4f, wn = %.4f rad/s; lat s = %.3f %+.3fi 1/s -> zeta = %.4f, wn = %.4f rad/s.\n\n', ...
    V.lon.match, V.lat.match, V.lon.cst_diff, V.lat.cst_diff, real(V.lon.s_dom), imag(V.lon.s_dom), V.lon.zeta_dom, V.lon.wn_dom, real(V.lat.s_dom), imag(V.lat.s_dom), V.lat.zeta_dom, V.lat.wn_dom);
fl('**Zero problem and its fix.** A PID has two zeros (roots of Nc). In a 1-DOF loop they produce a large overshoot even though the poles are well damped:');
fp('| loop | OS 1-DOF [%%] | rise 10-90 %% [s] | t_ss 1-DOF [s] |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| lon | %.1f | %.3f | %.2f |\n| lat | %.1f | %.3f | %.2f |\n\n', V.lon.m1.overshoot, V.lon.m1.rise, V.lon.m1.ts, V.lat.m1.overshoot, V.lat.m1.rise, V.lat.m1.ts);
fl('Fix (pole placement with zero cancellation, 2-DOF): reference prefilter `F(z) = Nc(1) z^2 / Nc(z)` (`pid_prefilt.m`; the poles of F are the PID zeros listed above, all inside the unit circle, DC gain 1, implemented as `c2 rf(k) = Nc(1) r(k) - c1 rf(k-1) - c0 rf(k-2)`). The reference transfer function becomes')
fl('```');
fl('T_r(z) = F(z) Nc(z) Np / Delta(z) = Nc(1) Np z^2 / ( Ad(z) R(z) )       (T_r(1) = 1; no zero other than z = 0)');
fl('```');
fl('so the step response is governed by the placed poles only. The feedback path (disturbance rejection, ramp tracking) is unchanged. The prefilter is used for reference steps (the metrics below); for a smoothly moving reference (navigation) the plain 1-DOF loop plus feedforward is used.');
fp('Check of the derivation: the closed-form T_r(z) above, evaluated with `filter`, agrees with the step response of the simulated difference equations (plant + `pid_step` + `pid_prefilt`) to %.1e (lon) and %.1e (lat).\n\n', V.lon.tr_diff, V.lat.tr_diff);
fl('');
fl('### Metrics vs targets (unit step, own difference-equation simulation `pid_linsim.m`, full-order model, with prefilter)');
fl('');
fl('| loop | metric | target | achieved | result |');
fl('|---|---|---|---|---|');
ln = {'lon', 'lat'};
for i = 1:2
    c = chk.(ln{i});
    fp('| %s | damping ratio of dominant poles | >= %.1f | %.3f | %s |\n', ln{i}, tg.zeta, c.zeta, ps(c.zeta >= tg.zeta));
    fp('| %s | 2 %% settling time | <= %.1f s | %.2f s | %s |\n', ln{i}, tg.ts, c.ts, ps(c.ts <= tg.ts));
    fp('| %s | overshoot | < %.0f %% | %.2f %% | %s |\n', ln{i}, tg.os, c.os, ps(c.os < tg.os));
    fp('| %s | 10-90 %% rise time | - | %.2f s | - |\n', ln{i}, c.rise);
end
fl('');
fl('Figures: `fig/fig_pz_map.png` (desired vs achieved poles, unit circle and zoom), `fig/fig_steps.png` (step responses with +/-2 % band, 10 % overshoot line and 2.5 s line).');
fl('');

fl('## 7. Saturation and anti-windup');
fl('');
fp('Limits: longitudinal `0 <= v_cmd <= %.1f m/s`; lateral `|delta| <= %.2f rad`, mapped to a speed-dependent `|u_lat| <= lat_input(v, delta_max)` (`pid_ulat_limit.m`; %.3f rad/s at v = 2 m/s, 0.86 at 1 m/s, 1.81 at 3 m/s). The feedforward is added *before* the limiter so the anti-windup acts on the total command.\n\n', par.v_max, par.delta_max, ga.umax);
fl('Implemented in `pid_step.m` (selectable, `g.aw`):');
fl('```');
fl('u_un = uff + Kp e + I(k-1) + Ki T e + D;   u = sat(u_un, umin, umax)');
fl('aw = 1 (default) back-calculation:  I(k) = I(k-1) + Ki T e(k) + T Kt (u - u_un),   Kt = 1/Tt = 1/sqrt(Ti Td)');
fl('aw = 2 conditional integration:     I(k) = I(k-1) if (u_un > umax and e > 0) or (u_un < umin and e < 0), else I(k-1) + Ki T e');
fl('aw = 0 none');
fl('```');
fl('In the unsaturated regime u = u_un exactly, so the linear design is untouched. Demonstration with large steps (1-DOF, no prefilter, `fig/fig_antiwindup.png`):');
fl('');
fl('| test | anti-windup | overshoot [%] | t_ss [s] | time at upper limit (lon) / at either limit (lat) [s] |');
fl('|---|---|---|---|---|');
for ia = 1:3
    AWN_ = {'none', 'back-calculation', 'clamping'}; nm_ = AWN_{ia};
    fp('| lon 20 m step, v_cmd in [0, 3] | %s | %.1f | %s | %.2f |\n', nm_, AW.lon(ia).os, sprintf_ts(AW.lon(ia).ts), AW.lon(ia).sat_time);
end
for ia = 1:3
    AWN_ = {'none', 'back-calculation', 'clamping'}; nm_ = AWN_{ia};
    fp('| lat 2 m step, u_lat limit +-%.2f rad/s (linear plant) | %s | %.1f | %s | %.2f |\n', ga.umax, nm_, AW.lat(ia).os, sprintf_ts(AW.lat(ia).ts), AW.lat(ia).sat_time);
end
fl('');
fp('Non-linear plant, 1.5 m lateral step at 2 m/s (no scheduling): without anti-windup the integrator state keeps the steering saturated and the car circles (heading error grows to %.0f rad); back-calculation: overshoot %.1f %%, t_ss %.2f s, max heading error %.2f rad; clamping: overshoot %.1f %%, t_ss %.2f s. Back-calculation is therefore the default (clamping is kept as an option).\n\n', ...
    AW.lat_nl(1).psimax, AW.lat_nl(2).os, AW.lat_nl(2).ts, AW.lat_nl(2).psimax, AW.lat_nl(3).os, AW.lat_nl(3).ts);
fl('Note (lon): after the overshoot v_cmd stays at its lower limit 0 (the car cannot reverse), so a residual position overshoot of a few cm cannot be corrected - a physical limit, not windup. Note (lat): the lateral type-2 plant + integrator loop is only conditionally stable under saturation - without anti-windup a 3 m step drives the linear loop with saturation unstable; this is why anti-windup is mandatory here.');
fl('');

fl('## 8. Non-linear plant verification');
fl('');
fl('Plant per SPEC: `v(k+1) = a v(k) + b v_cmd(k)`, `s(k+1) = s(k) + T v(k)`; lateral: yaw rate from the identified Gr difference equation (3-sample delay) driven by `u_lat = lat_input(v, delta)` with `delta = lat_inverse(u_lat_sat, v)`, `|delta| <= 0.34`, Frenet error dynamics `psi_e(k+1) = psi_e + T (r - v kappa)`, `e_y(k+1) = e_y + T v sin(psi_e)` (functions `pid_sim_lon.m`, `pid_sim_lat.m`). Figures `fig/fig_nonlinear_lon.png`, `fig/fig_nonlinear_lat.png`.');
fl('');
fl('| test | result |');
fl('|---|---|');
fp('| lon, 2 m position step from standstill (prefilter, limits 0..3 m/s) | OS %.2f %%, rise %.2f s, t_ss %.2f s, peak v_cmd %.2f m/s (limit 3.0) |\n', NL.lon_step_m.overshoot, NL.lon_step_m.rise, NL.lon_step_m.ts, NL.lon_step_m.vmax);
fp('| lon, 0.3 m step (no saturation) | OS %.2f %%, t_ss %.2f s (identical to the linear model up to the v_cmd >= 0 limit) |\n', NL.lon_small_m.overshoot, NL.lon_small_m.ts);
fp('| lon, trapezoid reference (1 m/s^2 up to 2.5 m/s) | with v_ref feedforward: max error %.2f cm, RMSE %.2f cm, steady-state error %.1e m; PID only: max error %.2f cm (steady-state %.1e m: type 2) |\n', 100*NL.trap.ff_max, 100*NL.trap.ff_rmse, NL.trap.ff_ss, 100*NL.trap.nf_max, NL.trap.nf_ss);
fp('| lat, 0.5 m path-offset (reference) step at 2 m/s | OS %.2f %%, rise %.2f s, t_ss %.2f s, max abs(delta) %.3f rad (limit 0.34), not saturated |\n', NL.lat_step_m.overshoot, NL.lat_step_m.rise, NL.lat_step_m.ts, NL.lat_step_m.dmax);
fp('| lat, 0.5 m initial offset, prefilter state initialised at the offset | zero crossing %.2f %% of offset, t_ss %.2f s, max abs(delta) %.3f rad |\n', NL.lat_init_m2.undershoot, NL.lat_init_m2.ts, NL.lat_init_m2.dmax);
fp('| lat, 0.5 m initial offset, plain 1-DOF regulation (no prefilter) | crosses zero: undershoot %.1f %% of the offset (PID zeros act on the disturbance response), t_ss %.2f s, max abs(delta) %.3f rad |\n', NL.lat_init_m1.undershoot, NL.lat_init_m1.ts, NL.lat_init_m1.dmax);
fp('| lat, constant-curvature arc kappa = 0.25 1/m at 2 m/s (R = 4 m) | max CTE / RMSE: with curvature feedforward %.1f cm / %.2f cm, PID only %.1f cm / %.2f cm (delta_ss = %.3f rad) |\n', 100*NL.arc.ff_max, 100*NL.arc.ff_rmse, 100*NL.arc.nf_max, 100*NL.arc.nf_rmse, NL.arc_ff.delta(end));
for iv = 1:3
    fp('| lat, 0.5 m step at v = %d m/s, scheduled gains | OS %.2f %%, t_ss %.2f s, max abs(delta) %.3f rad |\n', iv, NL.lat_v(iv).m.overshoot, NL.lat_v(iv).m.ts, NL.lat_v(iv).dmax);
end
fl('');
fl('Reading: the spec targets (OS < 10 %, t_ss < 2.5 s) also hold on the non-linear plant. The initial-offset case with the plain 1-DOF loop is the classical PID-zero effect (the regulation response of a type-3 loop crosses zero); engaging the prefilter state at the measured offset (what `pid_prefilt_init(e_y0)` does) removes it. With the lap-level requirement CTE_max <= 0.15 m / RMSE <= 0.05 m (scaled), the arc test (CTE 2.6 cm with curvature feedforward) leaves a large margin.');
fl('');

fl('## 9. Robustness');
fl('');
fp('Loop-gain multiplier q (Delta_q = Dc Dp + q Nc Np) for which the closed loop stays stable: lon %.3f ... %.2f (%.1f dB down / %.1f dB up), lat %.3f ... %.2f (%.1f dB down / %.1f dB up). Both loops are conditionally stable (type 2/3): too *little* gain is as dangerous as too much, which motivates the speed scheduling below.\n\n', ...
    R.lon.q_lo, R.lon.q_hi, 20*log10(1/R.lon.q_lo), 20*log10(R.lon.q_hi), R.lat.q_lo, R.lat.q_hi, 20*log10(1/R.lat.q_lo), 20*log10(R.lat.q_hi));
fl('| lat, gains fixed at the v0 = 2 m/s design | v [m/s] | max abs(pole) | zeta_dom | OS [%] | t_ss [s] |');
fl('|---|---|---|---|---|---|');
for iv = 1:3, fp('| unscheduled | %d | %.4f | %.3f | %.2f | %.2f |\n', Rv(iv).v, Rv(iv).fix_maxp, Rv(iv).fix_zd, Rv(iv).fix_os, Rv(iv).fix_ts); end
fp('| **scheduled (K ~ v0/v)** | 1, 2, 3 | identical (char. polynomial difference %.0e) | %.3f | %.2f | %.2f |\n\n', max([Rv.sch_diff]), V.lat.zeta_dom, V.lat.m2.overshoot, V.lat.m2.ts);
fl('Without scheduling the lateral loop is poorly damped at 1 m/s (loop gain halved) - scheduling is required for the 1-3 m/s range; with scheduling the linear loop is invariant and the non-linear plant gives OS ~1.2 %, t_ss ~2.1 s at 1, 2 and 3 m/s (Section 8).');
fl('');
fl('| other variations (linear, full order) | zeta_dom | OS [%] | t_ss [s] |');
fl('|---|---|---|---|');
for it = 1:3, fp('| lon lag time constant x%.1f (DC gain kept) | %.3f | %.2f | %.2f |\n', Rl(it).tau, Rl(it).zd, Rl(it).os, Rl(it).ts); end
for ig = 1:3, fp('| lat plant gain x%.1f (scheduled gains) | %.3f | %.2f | %.2f |\n', Rg(ig).gfac, Rg(ig).zd, Rg(ig).os, Rg(ig).ts); end
fl('');
fl('The lateral loop tolerates +30 % plant gain without loss, but a -30 % gain error (e.g. k_delta over-estimated) lowers the damping of the dominant pair to about 0.3 while the overshoot stays < 10 % and the loop remains stable; the identified k_delta already includes the speed-dependent correction of `lat_input`, so the residual gain uncertainty is expected to be far smaller (01_sysid: validation fit 91.7 %). Figure: `fig/fig_robust.png`.');
fl('');

fl('## 10. Use in the navigation package (04)');
fl('');
fl('```matlab');
fl('addpath(''../02_pid'', ''../common'');  load(''../02_pid/pid_gains.mat'');   % pid_lon, pid_lat');
fl('% longitudinal: e_s = s_ref - s_hat, feedforward v_ref (speed profile along the path)');
fl('st_lon = pid_init(e0);   [v_cmd, st_lon] = pid_step(s_ref - s_hat, st_lon, pid_lon, v_ref);');
fl('% lateral: e = 0 - e_y (+ left), feedforward u_ff = v*kappa/k_delta  (k_delta = pid_lat.k_delta = 1.7281)');
fl('g = pid_sched(pid_lat, v, 1);  lim = pid_ulat_limit(v, par);  g.umax = lim;  g.umin = -lim;');
fl('[u_lat, st_lat] = pid_step(0 - ey_hat, st_lat, g, v*kappa/pid_lat.k_delta);');
fl('delta = lat_inverse(u_lat, v, par);');
fl('```');
fl('- `pid_step.m` / `pid_init.m` / `pid_prefilt.m` / `pid_prefilt_init.m` / `pid_sched.m` carry `%#codegen`, use fixed-size scalars/struct fields only (state: I, ud, eprev, sat, uun). Code generation itself was not run here (no MATLAB Coder in the container).');
fl('- The feedforward gain must be `v*kappa/k_delta` (not `v*kappa`): Gr has DC gain k_delta = 1.728 on the corrected input u_lat.');
hfg = ga.Kp + ga.Ki*T/2 + 2*ga.Kd*(1 - ga.pf)/T/(1 + ga.pf);
fp('- Position/CTE inputs come from the LKF estimates; the filtered derivative acts on the estimated error, so noise on e_y at the Nyquist frequency is amplified by |C(-1)| = %.0f rad/s per m (lat, v0 = 2 m/s; scheduled gains scale with v0/v) and %.0f m/s per m (lon) - check once with the real LKF noise level.\n', hfg, gl.Kp + gl.Ki*T/2 + 2*gl.Kd*(1 - gl.pf)/T/(1 + gl.pf));
fl('- v < 1 m/s is outside the identified range; `pid_sched` clips v at 1 m/s.');
fl('');

fl('## 11. Files');
fl('');
fl('`02_pid/`: `pid_main.m` (runs everything), `pid_design.m` (gains from model + specs), `pid_diophantine.m` (exact placement, any plant order), `pid_coeffmatch.m` (closed-form 2nd-order matching, used as check and for the rejected reduction), `pid_dpoles.m`, `pid_clpoly.m`, `pid_step.m`, `pid_init.m`, `pid_prefilt.m`, `pid_prefilt_init.m`, `pid_sched.m`, `pid_ulat_limit.m`, `pid_linsim.m`, `pid_sim_lon.m`, `pid_sim_lat.m`, `pid_stepinfo.m`, `pid_domzeta.m`, `pid_pack.m`, `pid_write_results.m`, `pid_gains.mat` (`pid_lon`, `pid_lat` structs + `spec`, `design_space_lat`, `naive_static_reduction`, `antiwindup`), `fig/*.png` (200 dpi): `fig_pz_map`, `fig_steps`, `fig_antiwindup`, `fig_nonlinear_lon`, `fig_nonlinear_lat`, `fig_robust`.');
fl('');
fl('## 12. Caveats');
fl('');
fl('- The lateral t_ss = 2.1 s (target 2.5 s) is close to the limit of what a single PID can achieve on this plant (0.1 s equivalent delay, type 2 + integral action); larger damping slows the loop (Section 3).');
fl('- Step metrics are for the prefiltered (2-DOF) loop. The plain 1-DOF loop has large overshoot (lat 64 %, lon 22 %) on reference steps and ~51 % zero-crossing on an initial offset; the prefilter must be engaged for reference steps/initial offsets.');
fl('- The lateral linear model assumes constant v; speed changes are covered by gain scheduling and by the lat_input/lat_inverse static map (not by a time-varying proof). Below 1 m/s the identified model is not valid.');
fl('- Actuator rate limits (steering servo slew, VESC acceleration) are not modelled beyond the identified linear dynamics.');
fclose(fid);
end

function s = sprintf_ts(ts)
if isnan(ts), s = 'never (not settled)'; else, s = sprintf('%.2f', ts); end
end
